static const char* const kAboutTitle = "About Physics Lab";
static const char* const kAboutHtml = "<div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\"><b>Physics Lab</b></div><div class=\"lrnSm\">A playground for seeing how the universe works: real orbits, atoms, molecules and a black hole. It is built on real physics, and where something is simplified, it says so.</div></div><p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The developer:</b> Physics Lab is made by <b>one developer</b>, one update at a time. Please report any bug you find or idea you have.</p><p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Built with Claude:</b> The developer has the ideas and picks what goes in. Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, helps write the code, check the facts and test everything.</p><div class=\"lrnP srcBox\"><b>Where the facts come from:</b> planet positions come from NASA/JPL data. The isotope table is based on NUBASE nuclear data (through the mendeleev package). Guide facts are checked against several sources, and where they disagree the text says so. The main ones:<div class=\"srcH\">🪐 Space and orbits</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/approx_pos.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planet positions (Keplerian elements, 1800–2050)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/planetary/factsheet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: planetary fact sheet</a> (sizes, masses and temperatures; the planets&rsquo; gravity is from JPL, below)</li><li>IAU 2009 System of Astronomical Constants (Luzum et al. 2011, Celestial Mechanics and Dynamical Astronomy 110, 293): the Sun&rsquo;s mass is 332,946.0487 Earths (the Simple mode&rsquo;s centre of mass)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/tools/sbdb_lookup.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Small-Body Database</a> (dwarf planets, asteroids, comets)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/moons/phobos/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA Science: Phobos</a> (read 2026-11-07: it will crash into Mars or break up into a ring in about 50 million years)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/europa/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA Science: Europa</a> (read 2026-11-08: strong evidence of a saltwater ocean under the ice)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/planets/phys_par.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planetary physical parameters</a> (each planet&rsquo;s surface gravity at the equator, read from the page on 2026-10-03; the cards give it in g, with Earth&rsquo;s 9.80 m/s&sup2; on the same page as 1 g)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons, the Sun&rsquo;s header (Horizons API, COMMAND 10, read 2026-10-03): &ldquo;Solar radius (IAU2015) = 695700 km&rdquo;, the nominal solar radius the lab uses; and &ldquo;Adopted sid. rot. per. = 25.38 d&rdquo;, the Sun&rsquo;s standard turning period on its card</li><li>NASA/JPL Small-Body Database records of the six famous comets (SBDB API, read 2026-10-03: orbit 75 Halley, 226 Hale&ndash;Bopp, 31 NEOWISE, K273/20 Encke, 32 Swift&ndash;Tuttle; 67P: orbit K284/1 as Horizons gives it at 2021-11-02), and Horizons position vectors of the comets at two dates each, used to check them; the comets&rsquo; nucleus sizes are the Small-Body Database&rsquo;s diameters (it has none for NEOWISE). Each comet is one fixed two-body orbit, so it drifts from the real one away from the record&rsquo;s date</li><li>NASA/JPL news, &lsquo;Return of Halley&rsquo;s Comet&rsquo; (9 Jan 1985): its perihelion on 9 Feb 1986; JPL Horizons (orbit solution JPL#75, read 11 Nov 2026): the next on 28 July 2061</li><li>NASA NSSDC, &lsquo;Comet Hale-Bopp&rsquo; (D. R. Williams): its perihelion on 1 April 1997; JPL Horizons, Comet Swift&ndash;Tuttle (orbit solution JPL#32, read 11 Nov 2026): its perihelia on 12 Dec 1992 and 12 July 2126</li><li>F. Espenak, NASA, &lsquo;2012 Transit of Venus&rsquo; (eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov): the geocentric contacts and greatest transit, 01:29:36 UT on 6 June 2012, where the Time panel&rsquo;s jump lands</li><li>NASA/JPL astrodynamic parameters page, the astronomical unit: 149,597,870,700 m exactly, which the lab uses for every AU, and the speed of light, 299,792,458 m/s (quoted there as a 2018 CODATA value). The page names the IAU 2012 resolution as B1, the owner's decision calls it B2; the IAU text itself (and the SYRTE page about it) could not be opened, so it was not read. The lab used a rounded 149.6e9 m before 0.10.5 stage 4. A light year is worked out as that speed times a 365.25-day year, in AU (the IAU value was not looked up)</li><li>Figures with no named source, marked rough in the app or stated as the app's own rule: the Sun's surface temperature, habitable zone, frost line and lifetime; the Sun's 25-day equator and 34-day pole turning; the counts and masses on the belt cards (asteroid, Trojan, Kuiper, Oort); NEOWISE's 5 km nucleus; the 30 % reflected-light assumption for temperatures without an atmosphere; the Moon's phase names between the four main phases (this app's own cut-offs)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/phys_par/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: planetary satellite physical parameters</a> and <a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/elem/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">mean elements</a> (the moons: sizes and orbits)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/astro_par.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: astrodynamic parameters</a> (GM of the Sun and of each planet with its moons, DE440 values, for &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; and, from 0.46.7, the Sun&rsquo;s GM in Simple too; first read from an excerpt of the page, then read from the page itself and matched the code, 2026-10-03; 0.32: the Pluto system, 975.5 km&sup3;/s&sup2;, read 2026-11-06)</li><li>Park et al., 2021, The Astronomical Journal: the DE440 and DE441 planetary and lunar ephemerides (the paper behind those DE440 values)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd-api.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/horizons.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Horizons API documentation</a> (how the Horizons vector tables used for the start of &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; are asked for; 0.32: Pluto Barycenter (9), DE441, 1800&ndash;2100 yearly, read 2026-11-05, gives Pluto&rsquo;s start and its measured error; 0.36: Ceres, Haumea, Makemake and Eris the same way, JPL small-body orbit solutions JPL#48, #132, #130 and #103, read 2026-11-06, give their start and their measured errors)</li><li><a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/de440_de441.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: DE440 and DE441</a> (the note on the two ephemerides: Horizons&rsquo; default is DE441, which spans &minus;13200 to +17191 and is a little less accurate than DE440 for the present century, mainly for the Moon; the page was not opened in the session that wrote this, the figures are as the owner read them; the Real note&rsquo;s errors are against DE441, not DE440)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-10-03 (Horizons API, ephemeris DE441: position and velocity of the Sun and of each planet system, 1 January 12:00 TDB of every year from 1800 to 2100; the start of &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; is its 1 January 2026 row, the other rows check the motion)</li><li><a href=\"https://cdsarc.cds.unistra.fr/ftp/VI/79/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Chapront-Touz&eacute; and Chapront, 1988, Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics 190, 342: the lunar theory ELP2000-82B</a> (CDS catalogue VI/79: the series and the Fortran routine it is written from; the Moon&rsquo;s position, from the 487 largest of its 37,872 terms. It is a 1988 theory fitted to JPL&rsquo;s older DE200, so it is less accurate than JPL&rsquo;s own ephemerides: against Horizons (DE441) at the dates checked between 1800 and 2100 it is within 5.2 km (2.9 arcseconds) at worst. Real planet physics in the Orbit Lab draws Earth&rsquo;s Moon from it: its direction and phase are real, its distance is drawn exaggerated like every moon&rsquo;s; positions are geometric (no light time), dates are used as TDB, and outside 1800 to 1 Jan 2100 it is not used. The phase names: the four main phases are exact, the names in between (crescent, gibbous) use this app&rsquo;s own cut-offs, at 2, 40, 60 and 98 % lit)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-10-03, the Moon table (Horizons API, ephemeris DE441: the position of the Moon and the Sun seen from the Earth&rsquo;s centre, every 5 years from 1800 to 2100, daily for 60 days from 2025-12-02 and hourly on 2026-01-02 to 04; used only to check the ELP2000-82B Moon and its phases)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-10-03, the Earth table (Horizons API, ephemeris DE441: the position of the Earth&rsquo;s centre seen from the Sun&rsquo;s centre, 1 January 12:00 TDB of every year from 1800 to 2100; used only to check where Real planet physics puts Earth)</li><li>NASA/JPL Horizons data retrieved 2026-11-11, the Voyagers (Horizons API, COMMAND -31 and -32, the Sun's centre, ecliptic J2000): their place on 1 January of every year 1990 to 2026, joined by straight lines up to 30 Sep 2026 (data/jpl/voyager/)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA Science: Voyager 1</a> (on 17 Feb 1998, at 69.4 AU, Voyager 1 passed Pioneer 10 and became the most distant human-made object; read 2026-11-11)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/oort-cloud/facts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA Science: Oort Cloud Facts</a> (a spherical shell; inner edge 2,000 to 5,000 AU, outer edge 10,000 to 100,000 AU; hundreds of billions, even trillions, of icy bodies; read 2026-11-11)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1086/115978\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wisdom &amp; Holman, 1991: Symplectic maps for the N-body problem</a> (the method that moved the planets in &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; until 0.29; the tests still check it)</li><li>Hairer, N&oslash;rsett &amp; Wanner, 1993: <i>Solving Ordinary Differential Equations I</i>, 2nd edition, section II.9 (the Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer extrapolation method that moves the planets in &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo; since 0.29)</li><li><a href=\"https://iers-conventions.obspm.fr/content/chapter10/tn36_c10.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36 (Petit &amp; Luzum), chapter 10</a> (equation 10.12: the Sun&rsquo;s first-order relativistic pull behind &ldquo;General relativity (1PN)&rdquo; for the planets since 0.29)</li><li>Duncan, Levison &amp; Lee, 1998: A multiple time step symplectic algorithm for integrating close encounters (The Astronomical Journal; the variables that method is written in)</li><li>Bate, Mueller &amp; White, 1971: <i>Fundamentals of Astrodynamics</i> (moving a planet along its Kepler orbit with universal variables)</li><li>Clemence, 1947: The relativity effect in planetary motions (Reviews of Modern Physics; the 532 arcseconds per century of Mercury&rsquo;s perihelion advance from the other planets, used to check &ldquo;Planet physics: Real&rdquo;)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/dtamayo/reboundx/blob/main/src/gr_potential.c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">REBOUNDx, the file gr_potential.c</a> (Tamayo, Rein, Shi &amp; Hernandez, 2020, Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society 491, 2885: the simple potential of the Sun behind &ldquo;General relativity (1PN)&rdquo; for the planets until 0.29; the formula was read in that source file)</li><li>Nobili &amp; Roxburgh, 1986: Simulation of general relativistic corrections in long term numerical integrations of planetary orbits (IAU Symposium 114; the idea of that potential; the paper itself was not read)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.7377\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Will, 2014: The confrontation between general relativity and experiment</a> (Living Reviews in Relativity 17; Mercury&rsquo;s relativistic perihelion advance of 42.98 arcseconds per century, used to check &ldquo;General relativity (1PN)&rdquo;; read from a search excerpt, not from the paper)</li><li><a href=\"https://dlmf.nist.gov/14.30\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, section 14.30: spherical harmonics</a> (the definition and the Condon&ndash;Shortley phase of Y<sub>lm</sub> behind the complex orbitals m = +1, &minus;1, &hellip; in the Atom Lab; the page itself was not opened from the session that wrote this)</li><li>SciPy 1.17.1 (<code>scipy.special.sph_harm_y</code>) and scikit-image 0.26.0 (<code>skimage.color.lab2rgb</code>): used only in the tests, as independent checks of those spherical harmonics and of the phase colour wheel</li><li>CIE 1976 L*a*b* colour space and the sRGB standard (IEC 61966-2-1): the phase colour wheel is a circle of constant lightness in L*a*b*, so the colour shows the phase and not a brightness pattern (the formulas are the standard ones, written from knowledge, not looked up here)</li><li>Phase colours on the graphics card (0.13 stage 1): the same colour map written a second time in the shader language (GLSL 330), as a direct port of the C++ function and in 32-bit instead of 64-bit numbers; no new data or source, only who works out the colour changed. The tests check that its constants are the C++ ones, that glslang (the Khronos reference compiler) accepts every shader, and that pictures drawn by the old way (the processor colours each dot) and the new way (the graphics card picks the colour) with Mesa&rsquo;s software graphics (Vulkan, lavapipe, since 0.69.0; OpenGL, llvmpipe, before) are the same pixel for pixel (largest difference in the pictures tested: 0 of 255).</li><li>Superposition and photon jumps on the graphics card (0.13 stage 2): which dots of a superposition or a cloud jump are shown, and their colours, are now worked out by a compute shader (OpenGL 4.3 then; Vulkan since 0.61.0) from the amplitudes stored with each dot; where the card&rsquo;s program cannot be made the processor does it as before. No new physics and no new data: the formulas are the lab&rsquo;s own (the hydrogen-like wave functions and the quantum beats, from the textbooks listed above: Griffiths, <i>Introduction to Quantum Mechanics</i>, and Bethe and Salpeter), with the CODATA 2022 constants already used (Bohr radius 5.29177210544e-11 m, Hartree energy 27.211386245981 eV, proton-electron mass ratio 1836.152673426, h = 6.62607015e-34 J s exact). The tests check the superposed density, phase and beat period at fixed points against those textbook formulas written out separately, and the graphics-card result against the processor&rsquo;s for every dot, in 32-bit against 64-bit numbers: a dot within rounding of a threshold may come out differently (a handful in a million), and each such dot is checked to be that close. Found while checking: the lab normalised each orbital by a numerical sum that left its amplitude too big by 3e-6 (2p) to 1.6e-5 (3d z&sup2;) against the exact textbook value 1; made exact in 0.13 stage 3 (see below).</li><li>The 90 % outline on the graphics card, and the exact normalisation of the orbitals (0.13 stage 3). Normalisation: the integral of the squared wave function is now worked out in closed form. The radial part is (n/2)<sup>2l+3</sup> &middot; 2n &middot; (n+l)!/(n&minus;l&minus;1)!, from the orthogonality and the three-term recurrence of the generalised Laguerre polynomials (NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, chapter 18; Abramowitz and Stegun, chapter 22), and it is multiplied by the sphere integral of the square of each angular polynomial (the real cubic harmonics are combinations of the spherical harmonics, normalised as in Griffiths, <i>Introduction to Quantum Mechanics</i>, chapter 4). The formulas are the standard ones, written from knowledge; the chapter references were not opened in this session. They are checked by hand for 1s, 2p z, 3d z&sup2; and 4f z&sup3;, and by an independent quadrature for every orbital the app offers (real and complex, Z = 1, 2 and 5): each integrates to 1 within 1e-10 (before: off by up to 8.8e-5 in the density). Outline: the density on a 64&times;64&times;64 grid and its 90 % level (found by a search over the cell values instead of sorting them) are now worked out by compute shaders (OpenGL 4.3 then; Vulkan since 0.61.0) without the picture waiting, so the outline appears a frame or two after it is switched on; the processor does it where the card&rsquo;s program cannot be made. The region is defined as before (the densest cells holding 90 % of the probability, the standard textbook definition); the graphics-card level agrees with the processor&rsquo;s to 1e-4 and its cells to float rounding.</li><li>The Orbit Lab&rsquo;s rocks and the chart of nuclides on the graphics card (0.13 stage 4, the last of the four &ldquo;GPU update&rdquo; stages). No new data and no new physics. Rocks: where each belt rock is now worked out by a compute shader (OpenGL 4.3 then; Vulkan since 0.61.0) from its Kepler ellipse, with the same formulas as before: Kepler&rsquo;s equation M = E &minus; e sin E, then x&prime; = a (cos E &minus; e) and y&prime; = a &radic;(1 &minus; e<sup>2</sup>) sin E, and the mean motion n = &radic;(GM/a<sup>3</sup>) from Kepler&rsquo;s third law; they are the formulas of the NASA/JPL page &ldquo;Keplerian Elements for Approximate Positions of the Major Planets&rdquo; (listed above, re-read on 5 October 2026 for the first two). The time and the mean anomaly are kept in double precision on the graphics card and brought into &minus;&pi;..&pi; there; only that small angle goes into 32-bit numbers, so a rock is where it was after 10,000 years at the fastest time speed too: the tests compare every rock of the benchmark&rsquo;s scenes 1 and 6 with the processor&rsquo;s and with Kepler&rsquo;s equation solved separately by bisection, at times up to ten million years; the places agree to 0.003 of a point on the picture. Found while checking: the belt code solved Kepler&rsquo;s equation with exactly three Newton steps, which is exact for every rock the app makes (the largest eccentricity is 0.498) but not for e of 0.8 and more (0.45 radian off at e = 0.99); it is now iterated until converged, so the app&rsquo;s rocks do not move. Chart of nuclides: the 3,557 squares or blocks and the ground are made once and kept in a graphics-card buffer, and the tilt, zoom and pan are applied there; they are rebuilt only when the picture size, the theme colours or the height meaning changes. The pictures and the hit test agree with the old way (tested triangle by triangle, and by pictures).</li><li>The colour themes&rsquo; contrast rule (0.14 stage 1; the Frutiger Aero theme it was first written for was archived in 0.15 stage 5m). No new data and no physics. Contrast follows the W3C&rsquo;s Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 2.x (www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21, the definitions of &ldquo;relative luminance&rdquo; and &ldquo;contrast ratio&rdquo;, and success criteria 1.4.3 and 1.4.11): L = 0.2126 R + 0.7152 G + 0.0722 B, where each sRGB channel c (0 to 1) becomes c/12.92 if c &le; 0.04045, else ((c + 0.055)/1.055)<sup>2.4</sup>; the ratio is (L<sub>1</sub> + 0.05)/(L<sub>2</sub> + 0.05) with L<sub>1</sub> the lighter colour; it must be at least 4.5 for body text and 3 for large text and parts of the interface. Every text and background pair of the themes is computed with it, and the tests check the formula against values worked by hand (black on white 21, #777777 on white 4.48). The W3C page could not be opened from the session that wrote this, so the formula is written from knowledge and checked only by that hand arithmetic; older WCAG text says 0.03928 instead of 0.04045, which changes no 8-bit colour. Only the panels, buttons and text were checked: the pictures of the labs keep their own colours.</li><li>The Light accent fix (0.14 stage 2). No new data and no physics. Light&rsquo;s accent text (#2f6fed on its page #f7f7f5) was 4.24:1, under the 4.5 that body-size text needs; it is now #2d6ae3 (4.57:1), the same hue. The contrast rule is the same W3C WCAG 2.x formula as in the item above.</li><li><b>Font</b> of the New UI&rsquo;s look M1 (0.15 stages 5j and 5m): Inter (Ubuntu package fonts-inter 4.0+ds-1, &copy; 2016&ndash;2023 The Inter Project Authors, <a href=\"https://github.com/rsms/inter\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">github.com/rsms/inter</a>, SIL Open Font License 1.1). The Regular and SemiBold files are built into the app unchanged, and their licence text travels with the download.</li><li>Richard Fitzpatrick, <i>Introductory Quantum Mechanics</i> (lecture notes, University of Texas at Austin), the section on the 2P&ndash;1S transitions in hydrogen: the matrix element &lang;1s|z|2p&rang; = 128&radic;2/243 a<sub>0</sub> that sets the size of the oscillating dipole in the Atom Lab&rsquo;s quantum beats (the page was not opened from the session that wrote this; the number was checked by numerical integration in the tests)</li><li>CODATA 2022 recommended values (NIST): the Planck constant, the Rydberg energy and the proton&ndash;electron mass ratio, used in the tests to check the beat period h/(E<sub>2</sub>&minus;E<sub>1</sub>) (the values were read from the NIST pages on 2026-10-04, see the next entry)</li><li>David J. Griffiths, <i>Introduction to Quantum Mechanics</i>, the chapter on time-dependent perturbation theory (the two-level system): the textbook picture of a transition as a mixing of two states, which the Atom Lab&rsquo;s smooth photon jump follows. It is a picture, not a calculation of how a photon is absorbed (the book was not opened from the session that wrote this)</li><li>P. J. Mohr, D. B. Newell, B. N. Taylor and E. Tiesinga, &ldquo;CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022&rdquo;, <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.03787\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">arXiv:2409.03787</a> (saved as data/calc/): the calculator&rsquo;s G 6.674 30(15) &times; 10<sup>&minus;11</sup>, &epsilon;<sub>0</sub>, &mu;<sub>0</sub>, the exact N<sub>A</sub>, k, R and &sigma;, and the standard atmosphere; its other constants are the ones listed here. Since 0.50.1, from Table XXXIII of the same paper: the Bohr magneton &mu;<sub>B</sub> and the nuclear magneton &mu;<sub>N</sub>, the Hartree energy E<sub>h</sub> (the unit hartree), R<sub>&infin;</sub>, the electron&rsquo;s g-factor g<sub>e</sub> and anomaly a<sub>e</sub>, the proton&rsquo;s g-factor and the proton and neutron moments (2.792 847 344 63 and &minus;1.913 042 76 &mu;<sub>N</sub>, the same values the Atom Lab uses), the muon, deuteron and alpha-particle masses, the electron&rsquo;s Compton wavelength, the atomic units of length (bohr), time, electric field and electric dipole moment, the Fermi constant G<sub>F</sub>/(&hbar;c)<sup>3</sup> = 1.166 3787(6) &times; 10<sup>&minus;5</sup> GeV<sup>&minus;2</sup> (which CODATA takes from the Particle Data Group), and the exact flux quantum h/2e, von Klitzing constant h/e<sup>2</sup> and Josephson constant 2e/h. Units exact by definition: the debye 10<sup>&minus;21</sup>/c C&middot;m (c in m/s), the gauss 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> T, the erg 10<sup>&minus;7</sup> J and the barn 10<sup>&minus;28</sup> m<sup>2</sup>; spectroscopy conversions after &ldquo;to&rdquo; use the exact h, c and k (E = hc&nu;&#771;, E = hf, E = kT) and E = mc<sup>2</sup>.</li><li>The calculator&rsquo;s functions (0.50.1): the special functions and their conventions as in the NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions (dlmf.nist.gov: the gamma function ch. 5, erf ch. 7, Bessel functions ch. 10, the Ferrers (associated Legendre) functions with the Condon&ndash;Shortley phase ch. 14, the classical orthogonal polynomials and their recurrences ch. 18, 3j symbols ch. 34) and Racah&rsquo;s formula for the Wigner 3j symbol and Clebsch&ndash;Gordan coefficients (A. R. Edmonds, <i>Angular Momentum in Quantum Mechanics</i>); neither was opened from the session that wrote them: the tests check every function against closed forms, J<sub>0</sub>&rsquo;s first zero 2.404 825 557 695 773 found by bisection on its power series, and the Clebsch&ndash;Gordan coefficients against the closed forms for j<sub>2</sub> = 1/2 and their orthogonality. Since 0.50.2 the 3j sum is done exactly (whole numbers of any size and powers of primes), checked to 12 digits up to j = 100 against the closed forms of DLMF 34.3 (each first checked against sympy&rsquo;s exact values). The Zeeman formula uses Land&eacute;&rsquo;s g<sub>J</sub> with the measured electron g-factor (CODATA 2022; the formula as in Bransden &amp; Joachain, <i>Physics of Atoms and Molecules</i>, not opened from the session). The 2&pi; rules: E = h f with f in Hz, E = &hbar;&omega; with &omega; in rad/s, E = hc/&lambda; for a photon (h, c exact in the SI). The Atom Lab&rsquo;s formulas in the calculator are checked against the lab&rsquo;s own Dirac and hyperfine functions, NIST ASD&rsquo;s 2p&rarr;1s rate 6.2648 &times; 10<sup>8</sup> s<sup>&minus;1</sup> and 2p level energies, and the measured 21-cm line (Eides, Grotch &amp; Shelyuto 2001, eq. 406). Its astronomy: the IAU&rsquo;s au (2012 B2), parsec (2015 B2), nominal solar radius (2015 B3) and Julian year; its US units from the international yard and pound (1959)</li><li>Atoms in a magnetic field (0.54.0): the Landé g of the levels as NIST ASD lists them (the level pages with the Landé-g column: A. Kramida, Yu. Ralchenko, J. Reader and NIST ASD Team, NIST Atomic Spectra Database; read 2026-11-14, saved as received in data/jumps/asd_g); the Bohr magneton, μB/hc, the electron&rsquo;s g-factor and e/m from CODATA 2022 (Mohr et al., arXiv:2409.03787, Table XXXIII); the method: H. A. Bethe and E. E. Salpeter, <i>Quantum Mechanics of One- and Two-Electron Atoms</i> (1957), and E. U. Condon and G. H. Shortley, <i>The Theory of Atomic Spectra</i> (1935), the Zeeman-effect chapters; the 3j symbols and the Wigner&ndash;Eckart theorem as in A. R. Edmonds, <i>Angular Momentum in Quantum Mechanics</i> (1957); the matrix diagonalised by Jacobi rotations (G. H. Golub and C. F. Van Loan, <i>Matrix Computations</i>); the calculator&rsquo;s eigenvalues of small Hermitian matrices (0.54.2) use the same method with complex rotations, and the 2&times;2 closed form.</li><li>The Data panel (0.51): its tables are the labs&rsquo; own data, with the sources listed here; the ionisation limits of Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs and Fr for the quantum defects are NIST ASD&rsquo;s (the &ldquo;Limit&rdquo; row of each atom&rsquo;s saved level list) and R<sub>&infin;</sub> is CODATA 2022&rsquo;s. Fits: least squares by Householder QR, nonlinear ones by Levenberg&ndash;Marquardt (D. W. Marquardt, J. Soc. Ind. Appl. Math. 11, 431 (1963)); uncertainties in x by the effective variance (J. Orear, Am. J. Phys. 50, 912 (1982)), checked against York&rsquo;s straight line with errors in both variables on Pearson&rsquo;s data (D. York, N. M. Evensen, M. L. Mart&iacute;nez and J. De Basabe Delgado, Am. J. Phys. 72, 367 (2004)); start values of an exponential with a constant by the integral method (J. Jacquelin, <i>R&eacute;gressions et &eacute;quations int&eacute;grales</i> (2009)). The fitting is tested against the certified values of the NIST Statistical Reference Datasets for nonlinear regression (www.itl.nist.gov/div898/strd; the files as distributed, read from the copy in the lmfit-py repository because the NIST site refused the cloud session).</li><li>CODATA 2022 recommended values (NIST, the pages <code>physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?</code> followed by bohrrada0, hrev, aut, alph, rydhcev, mec2mev, meu, mpsme, ukg, muc2mev, me, mp, mn, mpu, mnu, k, h, c and e, read on 2026-10-04; each page says &ldquo;2022 CODATA recommended values&rdquo;): the Atom Lab&rsquo;s constants since 0.10.6 stage 2: the Bohr radius 5.29177210544&times;10<sup>&minus;11</sup> m, the Hartree energy 27.211386245981 eV, the atomic unit of time 2.4188843265864&times;10<sup>&minus;17</sup> s (so the atomic unit of speed), the fine-structure constant, the Rydberg energy 13.605693122990 eV, the electron mass energy, the electron, proton and neutron masses (in kg and in u), the atomic mass constant (in kg and in MeV), the proton&ndash;electron mass ratio, the Boltzmann constant, and the exact h, c and e (so h&middot;c = 1239.841984 eV&middot;nm). Before that the lab used rounded values, 0.529 &Aring;, 27.211 eV and 24.19 as (the Bohr radius was 0.03 % too small). The mass of the hydrogen-1 atom, 1.00782503223 u, is from NIST&rsquo;s isotope page (Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions, from the AME2020 mass evaluation), as in the Molecule Lab; the proton radius is CODATA 2022&rsquo;s 0.84075 fm. The tests compare the constants with these values typed in again from the pages, and check that the atomic unit of speed equals the fine-structure constant times c</li><li>Visible light, 380&ndash;780 nm: a convention this app uses; no source for the range was read. The Atom Lab uses this one range for the spectrum card, the colour of every line and the (UV) and (IR) tags (the spectrum card used 380&ndash;750 nm before 0.10.6 stage 5).</li><li>The Dirac 1s energy of a one-electron atom with a point nucleus, mc&sup2;(1 &minus; &radic;(1 &minus; (Z&alpha;)&sup2;)): used in the heavy-atom readout, and to choose the line (Z = 27, where it is about 1 % above the non-relativistic energy) from which the Atom Lab warns that its non-relativistic model is off. The formula is the standard one, written from knowledge, not looked up here; the percentages are worked out from it by the lab and the tests.</li><li>AME2020, the atomic mass evaluation (Huang, Wang, Kondev, Audi &amp; Naimi, 2021, Chinese Physics C 45, 030002; the table <code>www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/ame2020/mass.mas20.txt</code>, read on 2026-10-04): the binding energies the Atom Lab already used (3,557 nuclides) agree with its atomic masses, and since 0.10.6 stage 6 they give the nucleus&rsquo;s mass for the spectrum lines (its protons and neutrons, from CODATA 2022, minus its binding energy; before that A times the atomic mass unit, which was 0.70 % too light for deuterium and put its D-alpha line 1.3 pm too long). 695 of the nuclides on the neutron slider lie beyond the table and keep the old rough mass. The tests compare the masses with AME2020&rsquo;s atomic masses for H-1, H-2, H-3, He-4, C-12, Fe-56 and U-238.</li><li>NIST Atomic Spectra Database, lines form (<code>physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/ASD/lines1.pl</code>, read on 2026-10-04: the vacuum Ritz wavelengths of the fine-structure components of H I and He II): used only in the tests, to check that the lab&rsquo;s level-averaged, non-relativistic lines (Lyman-alpha, H-alpha, H-beta and two He II lines) lie within those components widened by (Z&alpha;)&sup2;, the size of what the lab leaves out (fine structure, the Lamb shift, spin).</li><li>NIST Atomic Spectra Database (ASD), lines form (<code>physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/ASD/lines1.pl</code>, retrieved on 2026-10-04, vacuum wavelengths, version 5.12 as listed on ASD&rsquo;s version history page): the real measured spectrum beside the lab&rsquo;s, in the Atom Lab&rsquo;s &ldquo;Real spectrum (NIST)&rdquo; card, as two separate comparisons. (a) The hydrogen-like ion of the element (H I, He II, &hellip; Fe XXVI: one electron round a nucleus of charge Z, the ion the lab models): the lines between levels n &le; 6, with the fine-structure components, to check the lab; they are observed wavelengths where ASD has them and otherwise Ritz wavelengths worked out from measured energy levels, each with the uncertainty ASD gives. (b) The neutral atom of the element (H I, He I, &hellip; Fe I): the 30 strongest observed lines in 100&ndash;2500 nm that ASD gives an intensity for (ASD says its relative intensities are qualitative, have no common scale and mean something only within one spectrum, so the lab ranks and draws them within one spectrum only), to show the limit of the lab, which has one electron where a real atom has Z. Spectra ASD does not have (the ions of Z = 111&ndash;118 and the neutral atoms of Fm and heavier) are said to be missing on screen. Since 0.10.8 stage 3 the same ASD lines also give the lab&rsquo;s lifetimes and jump rates: each line&rsquo;s transition probability A (the Einstein A coefficient, the spontaneous-emission rate, in s<sup>&minus;1</sup>), the accuracy code ASD gives it (AAA &le; 0.3 %, AA &le; 1 %, A+ &le; 2 %, A &le; 3 %, B+ &le; 7 %, B &le; 10 %, C+ &le; 18 %, C &le; 25 %, D+ &le; 40 %, D &le; 50 %, E &gt; 50 %; a prime means a multiplet was split assuming pure LS coupling) and its transition type (blank = allowed electric dipole E1; M1, E2, M1+E2, UT = forbidden), all kept from the same retrieval (no new request) and explained by ASD&rsquo;s lines help page, read on 2026-10-04, which also states ASD&rsquo;s own rule for an allowed line (levels of opposite parity, J changing by at most 1). The lab adds only the lifetime 1/&Sigma;A and the branching shares A/&Sigma;A over the allowed jumps to a lower n, and gives no number where ASD has none (no rate is calculated). In that data ASD lists the 1s&ndash;2s line of Cs LV (Z = 55) as allowed although l does not change; the lab follows the selection rule and does not use that value. Cited as NIST asks: Kramida, A., Ralchenko, Yu., Reader, J. and NIST ASD Team (2024). NIST Atomic Spectra Database (version 5.12), [Online]. Available: https://physics.nist.gov/asd. National Institute of Standards and Technology, Gaithersburg, MD. DOI: https://doi.org/10.18434/T4W30F. The Dirac energy formula the tests use to explain the heavy ions&rsquo; difference is the standard one, written from knowledge, not looked up here.</li><li>The Dirac energy levels of a one-electron atom with a point nucleus (the Sommerfeld fine-structure formula, E = mc&sup2;[(1 + (Z&alpha;)&sup2;/(n &minus; &delta;)&sup2;)<sup>&minus;1/2</sup> &minus; 1], &delta; = (j + &frac12;) &minus; &radic;((j + &frac12;)&sup2; &minus; (Z&alpha;)&sup2;)): the Atom Lab&rsquo;s &ldquo;Relativity (Dirac levels)&rdquo; setting (0.10.8, off by default) uses it for the energy levels and lines of the two spectrum cards. The formula is standard textbook material (Hans A. Bethe and Edwin E. Salpeter, <i>Quantum Mechanics of One- and Two-Electron Atoms</i>, listed in this section; Griffiths gives its expansion); it was written from knowledge and not looked up in the session that wrote it, and the tests check it against hand arithmetic, its expansion and the measured NIST lines (the ASD source above: the lab&rsquo;s Dirac lines are matched to ASD&rsquo;s components by n and j, and they land closer than the non-relativistic lines for H I, He II and Fe XXVI). The constants are the CODATA 2022 ones above. The nucleus&rsquo;s motion enters only through the reduced mass in the main energy; the relativistic correction is that of an infinitely heavy nucleus, and no source for the further recoil terms was read. Not in it: the Lamb shift and other QED effects, and the size of the nucleus.</li><li>The nucleus&rsquo;s Density look (0.22): a two-parameter Fermi (Woods&ndash;Saxon) charge distribution, the form fitted to electron-scattering charge densities, with diffuseness a = 0.54 fm (surface thickness 4 a ln 3, about 2.4 fm from 90 % to 10 %), scaled to the IAEA rms charge radius and drawn with the lab&rsquo;s measured shape: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.12785\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">arXiv:2104.12785</a>. The nucleons&rsquo; speed (about a quarter of light speed): the Fermi momentum of nuclear matter, k<sub>F</sub> &asymp; 1.33 fm<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; 262 MeV/c (v/c &asymp; 0.27), <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.0838\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">arXiv:0802.0838</a>.</li><li>Nuclear charge radii, the size every nucleus with a measurement is drawn at (0.10.9): the IAEA Nuclear Data Section&rsquo;s table of nuclear charge radii, the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/radii/charge_radii.csv</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 28,869 bytes). It has 957 rows: 908 isotopes of 90 elements, from hydrogen to curium (Z = 96), with a measured rms charge radius and its uncertainty; 48 more with only a preliminary value, which the lab does not use; and the neutron&rsquo;s row, a negative number (so a mean-square radius in fm&sup2;, not a radius), which the lab leaves out. The file carries no citation text and does not say which edition it is. The compilation behind a table like it is I. Angeli and K. P. Marinova, <i>Atomic Data and Nuclear Data Tables</i> 99 (2013) 69, &ldquo;Table of experimental nuclear ground state charge radii: An update&rdquo; (the title and its 909 isotopes of 92 elements were read from a search listing; the volume and page were not checked on the journal&rsquo;s page, and neither was which edition the IAEA file is). A charge radius is the rms radius of the protons&rsquo; electric charge. Neutrons carry no charge, so it is not the matter radius, and that is why halo nuclei are not drawn from it. The lab draws the nucleus as a uniform ball of radius R = &radic;(5/3) &times; the rms radius (a uniform ball has rms radius &radic;(3/5) R, a calculus result; that the nucleus is a uniform ball is an assumption, real nuclei have soft edges), and the nucleon positions are scaled so that the protons&rsquo; rms radius is the measured one. Where the table has no value the usual rule r = 1.2 A<sup>1/3</sup> fm is kept and the card says so. Hydrogen-1 keeps the CODATA 2022 proton radius above. That helium-4 is smaller than helium-3 and that lithium&rsquo;s radius falls to lithium-9 and rises at lithium-11 are the table&rsquo;s own numbers; the binding energies beside them are the AME2020 ones above. The relation R = &radic;(5/3) &times; rms was written from knowledge and not looked up.</li><li>Nuclear electric quadrupole moments, where the nucleus&rsquo;s shape comes from (0.10.9 stage 2): the IAEA Nuclear Data Section&rsquo;s recommended values, the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/nuclearmoments/elec_mom_recomm.csv</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 135,013 bytes, 1,155 rows: 599 ground states and 556 isomers; the lab uses the ground states only). Each row names the INDC report indc-nds-0833 and an NSR reference key; the page that states how to cite the table could not be read (a bot challenge), so no further citation is given. The file&rsquo;s second column, headed n.n+n.z, is the mass number (read that way every ground-state row is a known nuclide; read as a neutron count only 53 of 599 are). Values are in barns. Seven rows that are not one clean number (five of them typing errors), 41 values with no sign, four with a tentative sign and 34 with a tentative spin are not used for the shape; a duplicate K-41 row is left out. The table gives the spectroscopic moment, which is zero for spin 0 and 1/2 whatever the shape. The lab turns it into the intrinsic moment with the strong-coupling relation (K = I), Q = Q<sub>0</sub> I(2I &minus; 1) / ((I + 1)(2I + 3)): a model assumption, not a measurement, and a poor one for light nuclei and near closed shells; it is not used where it would need |&beta;<sub>2</sub>| above 0.6 (the lab&rsquo;s own limit, no source). The shape is a uniformly charged body r = R(1 + &beta;<sub>2</sub>Y<sub>20</sub>) whose volume is kept (an assumption), and &beta;<sub>2</sub> is solved from Q<sub>0</sub> and the measured charge radius above. Since 0.46.3 a moment is turned into a shape only where FRDM(2012) (below) calculates a deformed nucleus, |&beta;<sub>2</sub>| at least 0.10 (the lab&rsquo;s own threshold): near closed shells the rule does not hold. Where there is no usable moment the lab uses a measured B(E2) (below), else FRDM(2012)&rsquo;s calculated &beta;<sub>2</sub>, else draws the nucleus round, and the card says which. The relation and the conversion were written from knowledge, not looked up.</li><li>B(E2; 0<sup>+</sup> &rarr; 2<sup>+</sup>) of the isotopes drawn from it (0.28; since 0.46.3 all 167 even-even isotopes with A &ge; 12 found in nature that ENSDF gives one for, read 2026-11-08): ENSDF, the Evaluated Nuclear Structure Data File, read through the IAEA&rsquo;s LiveChart API (<code>nds.iaea.org/relnsd/v1/data?fields=gammas</code>, 2026-11-05; each file names its evaluators), B(E2)&darr; in Weisskopf units turned into B(E2)&uarr; = 5 &times; B(E2)&darr; with B<sub>W</sub>(E2) = 0.0594 A<sup>4/3</sup> e<sup>2</sup> fm<sup>4</sup>. The sign of the deformation is the opposite of the sign of the measured quadrupole moment of the same 2<sup>+</sup> state in the IAEA table above; where that moment is not measured, the sign is FRDM(2012)&rsquo;s calculated one, and where FRDM has none or calls it round, the nucleus is drawn stretched and the card says the sign is unknown. B(E2) gives the size of the deformation, static and vibrating together: for a nearly round nucleus it is mostly a vibration. &beta;<sub>2</sub> = 4&pi;/(3ZR<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>) &radic;B(E2)&uarr; with R<sub>0</sub> = 1.2 A<sup>1/3</sup> fm.</li><li>Nuclear spins, the kind of each half-life and the chart of nuclides (0.11 stage 2): NUBASE2020, F.G. Kondev, M. Wang, W.J. Huang, S. Naimi and G. Audi, Chin. Phys. C45, 030001 (2021), the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/ame2020/nubase_4.mas20.txt</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 761,906 bytes; 3,558 ground-state lines: the free neutron and the 3,557 nuclides the chart draws, the same ones the lab already had). The half-lives, decay modes and odds on the chart come from the lab&rsquo;s own table (also NUBASE2020, through the mendeleev package); the file adds the spin and parity of each nuclide, with NUBASE&rsquo;s own marks (directly measured, estimated from systematics, tentative), and tells apart a half-life that is only an extrapolation from systematics (369 nuclides, never shown as a number), a limit, an approximate value and none. The page that states how the table may be cited could not be read (a bot challenge), so the credit is only the citation the file gives. The file&rsquo;s decay column marks some entries with a question mark and does not explain the mark in its header; the app shows such entries as written. The colour of a square is a convention of this lab, not a source: stable where the lab&rsquo;s table says stable (251; NUBASE2020 says stable for 253, and the two extra, krypton-78 and xenon-124, have measured half-lives in the lab&rsquo;s table), otherwise the kind of decay with the largest total odds (a beta-delayed branch counts as its beta); where the odds are not measured, the kind if all listed modes agree, else grey. The cross-check against the file&rsquo;s own decay column agrees for 3,054 of the 3,083 nuclides where it gives usable numbers; in most of the rest the lab&rsquo;s table fills an unquantified remainder that NUBASE marks with a question mark. The magic numbers 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 and 126 (filled shells in the nuclear shell model) were written from knowledge and not looked up; 126 is a neutron number only, since the chart ends at 118 protons.</li><li>The heights of the chart&rsquo;s landscape (0.11 stage 3) are a chosen scale, not a measurement and not from a source: the logarithm (base 10) of the half-life in seconds, from 10<sup>&minus;23</sup> s (flat) to 10<sup>32</sup> s (the top), which are the shortest and the longest half-lives in the lab&rsquo;s table rounded outwards to whole powers of ten; one scale height is drawn 12 tiles tall, a number chosen by looking so that the proton-rich side is not hidden behind the stable ridge. Stable nuclides have no half-life and all stand at one fixed height, 1.1 scale heights, above the top; a nuclide with no measured half-life, or only NUBASE&rsquo;s extrapolation from systematics, has no height at all (a flat hollow or pale square); a limit or an approximate value is drawn as a pale block at the limit&rsquo;s height. The projection is isometric: the chart is turned 45 degrees and tipped 54.74 degrees (arccos of 1/&radic;3) away from straight down, the standard angles that make the three axes look equally long; they were written from knowledge and not looked up.</li><li>Binding energy per nucleon, the chart&rsquo;s second height (0.11 stage 4): AME2020, the file <code>www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/ame2020/mass.mas20.txt</code> (retrieved on 2026-10-04, 476,239 bytes, last modified 7 March 2021), cited as the file itself gives it: W.J. Huang, M. Wang, F.G. Kondev, G. Audi and S. Naimi, Chin. Phys. C45, 030002 (2021), and M. Wang, W.J. Huang, F.G. Kondev, G. Audi and S. Naimi, Chin. Phys. C45, 030003 (2021). The file marks a value that is only an estimate (non-experimental) with a #: 1,008 of the 3,557 nuclides, among them every nuclide above Z = 110, whose masses have not been measured. Those are drawn flat and pale, never as a height. The lab&rsquo;s own binding table agrees with the file to 1 keV for all 3,557 nuclides. The height is a chosen linear scale, 0 to 9,000 keV per nucleon (not the total, which would only grow with the mass number); the highest measured value is read from the table, not typed: nickel-62, 8,794.56 keV, then iron-58 and iron-56. The sentence that fusing nuclei below the peak releases energy and splitting nuclei above it does is standard nuclear physics, written from knowledge and not looked up.</li><li>The valley and the island of stability (0.11 stage 4): the valley of stability is the band of stable nuclides that the chart already draws, so it is measured (the lab&rsquo;s table, NUBASE2020). The island of stability is a theoretical prediction, and no source for it could be read from these sessions: every host tried (Wikipedia, arXiv, INSPIRE, Crossref, journal sites and laboratory sites) answered with a connection refusal from the session&rsquo;s proxy (HTTP 403). So the app draws no predicted region and quotes no predicted number. It says only that theory expects such a region beyond the heaviest known nuclides (Z = 118 in NUBASE2020), that none of it has been observed, that this lab has no sourced prediction to draw, and that where it lies is disputed; that wording follows the project owner&rsquo;s brief and could not be checked against a source.</li><li>Decay chains on the chart (0.12 stage 1): every half-life, decay mode and branching odds are the lab&rsquo;s table (NUBASE2020 through the mendeleev package, above). The move of each mode on the chart is nuclear bookkeeping (an alpha takes away 2 protons and 2 neutrons, a beta minus turns a neutron into a proton, a beta plus or electron capture does the opposite), written from knowledge. The four classic series (uranium-238 to lead-206, uranium-235 to lead-207, thorium-232 to lead-208, neptunium-237 to thallium-205) were typed into the tests from standard textbook tables, <b>not</b> from a source read in the session that wrote them: no online source could be reached (every host answered with the session&rsquo;s proxy denial). What the tests check without any table is the mass-number rule (an alpha takes 4 off A and a beta changes only Z, so every member of a series has A = A<sub>0</sub> mod 4), the charge and mass count at the end of each series, the exponential law (the median lifetime is the half-life, the mean is the half-life divided by ln 2) and binomial statistics for the random walk. The walk uses the listed odds added up to 1. Where NUBASE2020 marks the odds as not measured the lab&rsquo;s table lists equal shares, and the chain uses those only as a marked stand-in, never as data. A nuclide whose half-life is only a limit, only an extrapolation from systematics, or not given is never given a time: the chain stops there and says so. The time on the screen is not real time: each step&rsquo;s lifetime is a random draw from the exponential law with the real half-life, and the steps are shown about 1.6 seconds apart whatever the drawn lifetimes were.</li><li>Accuracy clean-up of the half-lives and decay modes (0.12 stage 2). Every half-life in the Atom Lab is now checked against NUBASE2020, the file named above: all 2,799 plain values agree with it to 0.5 % (the lab writes three digits). To find where the lab&rsquo;s older table came from, the <code>mendeleev</code> package, version 1.3.0 from PyPI (read on 2026-10-04), was opened: it agrees with the older table for 2,774 of 2,799 half-lives, the others being superheavy nuclides. What changed: (1) 113 superheavy half-lives that the older table had rounded and marked approximate are now NUBASE2020&rsquo;s own measured values (the lab can no longer say &ldquo;approximate&rdquo; about them); (2) 16 more superheavy values were an older evaluation (up to 2.8 times different, for example seaborgium-269: 14 minutes before, 5 minutes now) and are now NUBASE2020&rsquo;s; (3) krypton-78 and xenon-124 had half-lives (9.2&times;10<sup>21</sup> and 1.8&times;10<sup>22</sup> years) that are in neither NUBASE2020 nor <code>mendeleev</code>, which both call them stable (NUBASE2020 with lower limits &gt;110 Ey and &gt;200 Ty); no source for those numbers is in this repository and none could be read, so they were removed and the two are shown as NUBASE2020 shows them, as observationally stable. Nuclides with no half-life number are described by what NUBASE2020 says (an extrapolation from systematics, only a limit, only an approximate value, &ldquo;p-unst&rdquo;, nothing): the text &ldquo;seen only fleetingly&rdquo; was not in the data. The decay clock times only nuclides with a measured or approximate half-life; the half-lives the older table held for 369 extrapolated nuclides and 11 limits are not used. The decay modes that NUBASE2020 lists with a question mark and no intensity (the file&rsquo;s header does not define the question mark) are said to be left out of the odds, for the 644 nuclides where that is so. Calculated shapes (0.46.3): <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.06294\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">P. M&ouml;ller, A. J. Sierk, T. Ichikawa, H. Sagawa, Nuclear ground-state masses and deformations: FRDM(2012), Atomic Data and Nuclear Data Tables 109&ndash;110, 1 (2016)</a>, the ground-state &beta;<sub>2</sub> of its 9,318 nuclei (oxygen to Z = 136), used for every nucleus without a measured B(E2); it replaced the lab&rsquo;s own unpublished estimate (Casten&rsquo;s P-factor with a curve of its own, and the unsourced magic numbers 114 and 184). The textbook magic numbers 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 and 126 were not looked up.</li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">⚛️ Atoms and particles</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/2024/reviews/rpp2024-rev-probability.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Particle Data Group 2024, the review Probability</a> (0.40: a Poisson count of mean &nu; has variance &nu;, so a part of the electron cloud holding n dots is uncertain by about 1/&radic;n; why the whole atom now keeps 12,000 dots per electron, up to 60,000 / 120,000 / 240,000; read 2026-11-07)</li><li><a href=\"https://access.archive-ouverte.unige.ch/access/metadata/cec3e8fd-d549-4235-a875-5e4078d67a4d/download\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">de Silva, Korchowiec and Wesolowski 2014, J. Chem. Phys. 140, 164301</a> (&ldquo;the electron density of atoms is a monotonically decaying function&rdquo;, conjectured: so the whole atom&rsquo;s cloud shows its shells by their colours and by the radial distribution, not as rings of density; read 2026-11-07)</li><li>The antimatter step of the timeline: P. A. M. Dirac, &ldquo;Quantised singularities in the electromagnetic field&rdquo;, Proc. R. Soc. A 133 (1931) 60 (the anti-electron predicted); C. D. Anderson, &ldquo;The positive electron&rdquo;, Phys. Rev. 43 (1933) 491 (found in 1932)</li><li>The oganesson step of the timeline: N. T. Brewer et al., &ldquo;Search for the heaviest atomic nuclei among the products from reactions of mixed-Cf with a <sup>48</sup>Ca beam&rdquo;, Phys. Rev. C 98 (2018) 024317 (a new <sup>294</sup>Og decay chain; three or four were seen by 2006)</li><li><a href=\"https://ajit.ext.unb.ca/download.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hartree-Fock orbitals of the atoms: T. Koga, K. Kanayama, S. Watanabe and A. J. Thakkar, Int. J. Quantum Chem. 71, 491 (1999), helium to xenon; T. Koga, K. Kanayama, T. Watanabe, T. Imai and A. J. Thakkar, Theor. Chem. Acc. 104, 411 (2000), caesium to lawrencium</a> (0.39 and 0.40: every electron of the neutral atom; since 0.41 the tests' reference for the non-relativistic limit)</li><li><a href=\"http://www.few.vu.nl/~visscher/FiniteNuclei/FiniteNuclei.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">L. Visscher and K. G. Dyall, Dirac-Fock atomic electronic structure calculations using different nuclear charge distributions, At. Data Nucl. Data Tables 67, 207 (1997)</a> (the Gaussian nucleus and the reference for every orbital of the app&rsquo;s Dirac-Hartree-Fock atoms, Electrons: All, since 0.41; the computation itself is the app&rsquo;s own, native/tools/dhf)</li><li><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/PhysRefData/ASD/levels_form.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Atomic Spectra Database, levels and lines of Li I, Na I, K I, Rb I and Cs I</a> (retrieved 8 Nov 2026, native/data/asd/alkali), and of Fr I (retrieved 9 Nov 2026, native/data/jumps/asd; since 0.47.2): the measured levels, vacuum wavelengths and transition rates A of the outer electron&rsquo;s jumps, Electrons: All, since 0.46</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/9906044\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">M. S. Safronova, W. R. Johnson and A. Derevianko, Phys. Rev. A 60, 4476 (1999)</a> (the frozen-core Dirac-Hartree-Fock method of the outer electron&rsquo;s orbitals, and the check of the app&rsquo;s computation against its Table I: K 4s, Rb 5s, Cs 6s; since 0.46; since 0.47.2 only the Non-relativistic model)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2018.10.032\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">C. Froese Fischer, G. Gaigalas, P. J&ouml;nsson and J. Biero&#324;, &ldquo;GRASP2018: a Fortran 95 version of the General Relativistic Atomic Structure Package&rdquo;, Comput. Phys. Commun. 237, 184 (2019)</a> (github.com/compas/grasp, MIT licence, commit 9006157): the program that computed every cloud of lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium and francium, Electrons: All (core-valence MCDHF and CI with the Breit interaction and QED; natural orbitals and the multipoles of the aligned state; native/tools/grasp; since 0.47.2), and since 0.47.3 the clouds of the levels of helium, beryllium, magnesium, zinc and cadmium (two outer s electrons), since 0.47.4 also calcium, strontium, barium and mercury (one orbital set per atom), since 0.51.3 radium (the element jumps are paused after it: owner, 14 Nov 2026), and the app&rsquo;s own transition rates where NIST has none (rtransition; A from the computed line strength with NIST&rsquo;s wavelength and NIST ASD&rsquo;s conversion factor)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.2578\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">M. S. Safronova, M. G. Kozlov, W. R. Johnson and D. Jiang, Phys. Rev. A 80, 012516 (2009)</a> (CI+all-order energies of magnesium's levels, Table II: the independent check of the app's magnesium clouds; since 0.47.3; since 0.47.4 also calcium, cadmium and barium)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.01885\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">V. A. Yerokhin and V. M. Shabaev, Lamb shift of n = 1 and n = 2 states of hydrogenlike atoms, 1 ≤ Z ≤ 110, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 44, 033103 (2015), arXiv:1506.01885</a> (the Lamb shift of 1s, 2s, 2p1/2 and 2p3/2 in the Dirac levels and lines of the one-electron atom; native/data/lamb; since 0.49.3)</li><li>V. B. Berestetskii, E. M. Lifshitz and L. P. Pitaevskii, Quantum Electrodynamics (Landau and Lifshitz vol. 4), section 36 (the closed-form radial functions of the Dirac equation in a Coulomb field: the Dirac clouds of the one-electron atom, since 0.49.3; the app checks them against the Dirac equation itself)</li><li>V. F. Weisskopf and E. Wigner, Z. Phys. 63, 54 (1930) (the exponential decay of an excited level by one photon: how the app draws a photon jump, since 0.49.3)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0409271\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">U. D. Jentschura, Two-Loop Bethe Logarithms for Higher Excited S Levels, arXiv:hep-ph/0409271 (2004)</a> (eq. 11: the two-photon decay of the metastable 2s level, 0.1215 s ÷ Z⁶, quoting Shapiro and Breit 1959 and Klarsfeld 1969; native/data/twophoton; since 0.46.1)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/nucl-th/0202015\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">L. R. Chamon et al., Phys. Rev. C 66, 014610 (2002)</a> (the point-nucleon diffuseness 0.50 fm of the drawn nucleons, since 0.46)</li><li><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?rd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CODATA 2022, deuteron rms charge radius 2.12778(27) fm</a> (the deuteron&rsquo;s drawn size, since 0.46; the IAEA table&rsquo;s 2.1421(88) fm is still shown beside it)</li><li><a href=\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/2rw6g1z7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">P. S. Bagus, Y. S. Lee and K. S. Pitzer, Effects of relativity and of the lanthanide contraction on the atoms from hafnium to bismuth, Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory report LBL-3787 (April 1975), submitted to Chem. Phys. Lett.</a> (gold&rsquo;s relativistic and non-relativistic orbitals: a test reference)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.02553\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">O. R. Smits, P. Indelicato, W. Nazarewicz, M. Piibeleht and P. Schwerdtfeger, Pushing the limits of the periodic table &mdash; a review on atomic relativistic electronic structure theory and calculations for the superheavy elements, Phys. Rep. 1035 (2023), doi:10.1016/j.physrep.2023.09.004 (arXiv:2301.02553)</a> (the predicted ground configurations and levels of meitnerium to oganesson; section 6.6: copernicium expected to act like a noble gas, oganesson possibly a solid semiconductor, which is why the families of rutherfordium to oganesson are shown as &ldquo;expected&rdquo;)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.11357\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">M. Lesiuk and B. Jeziorski, Diamagnetic susceptibility of neon and argon including leading relativistic effects, arXiv:2311.11357 (2023)</a> (how much electron correlation changes the size of light atoms: the size of the many-electron model&rsquo;s largest missing effect)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.14101\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">K. Sunaga, M. Salman and T. Saue, J. Chem. Phys. 157, 164101 (2022)</a> (QED corrections to gold&rsquo;s orbitals: the size of a missing effect)</li><li><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/PhysRefData/ASD/ionEnergy.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Atomic Spectra Database 5.12, ionization energies (Kramida, Ralchenko, Reader and NIST ASD Team, 2024, doi:10.18434/T4W30F)</a> (ground configurations, ground levels and first ionization energies, with NIST's marks for measured, semi-empirical and theoretical values)</li><li><a href=\"https://www-nds.iaea.org/amdc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NUBASE and the Atomic Mass Evaluation</a> (isotopes, half-lives, binding energy)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1088/1674-1137/abddae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NUBASE2020 (Kondev et al., 2021)</a> (how each unstable nucleus decays and the odds of each way, for radioactive decay in the Atom Lab)</li><li><a href=\"https://www-nds.iaea.org/nuclearmoments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IAEA table of recommended nuclear magnetic dipole moments</a> (the nuclei&rsquo;s spins and magnetic moments, for the hyperfine structure in the Atom Lab&rsquo;s Spectrum tab, since 0.31)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0002158\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Eides, Grotch &amp; Shelyuto, 2001: Theory of light hydrogenlike atoms</a> (Physics Reports 342; the hyperfine formula and the measured hydrogen and deuterium lines the tests compare with; 0.33: Breit&rsquo;s relativistic factor, eq. 274)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/9706031\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Shabaev, Tomaselli, K&uuml;hl, Artemyev &amp; Yerokhin, 1997: Ground state hyperfine splitting of high Z hydrogenlike ions</a> (Physical Review A 56, 252; the relativistic factor for 15 heavy ions, the nucleus&rsquo;s size, and hydrogen-like holmium-165 measured at 572.79 nm, which the tests compare with)</li><li><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?ae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CODATA 2022: the electron magnetic moment anomaly</a> (in the hyperfine splitting of s levels)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/lmmentel/mendeleev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The mendeleev Python package</a> (the data table the isotopes came through)</li><li>Melting points, boiling points and densities of the elements (0.28): W. M. Haynes (ed.), CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics, 97th edition (2016) for the temperatures and 95th edition (2014) for the densities, as stored in the database of the mendeleev package, version 1.3.0. Phosphorus is white phosphorus, sulfur the monoclinic form; carbon and arsenic sublime. Where the CRC table gives no boiling point the card says so; predicted densities (astatine, francium, einsteinium and heavier) are not shown. Checked against the ITS-90 temperature scale&rsquo;s fixed points (gallium, indium, tin, zinc, aluminium, silver, gold, copper, mercury, argon).</li><li><a href=\"https://pdg.lbl.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Particle Data Group</a> (quark masses: PDG 2024, up 2.16 &plusmn; 0.07 and down 4.70 &plusmn; 0.07 MeV, MS-bar scheme at 2 GeV; its review of quark and lepton compositeness for the quark size: contact-interaction limits of about 24 TeV, so a quark is smaller than about 8&times;10<sup>&minus;21</sup> m, and the quark model review: baryons are colour singlets, so quark colours cannot be observed)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.08677\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Y.-B. Yang et al., PRL 121, 212001 (2018)</a> (lattice QCD: the proton mass split at 2 GeV, quark energy 32 %, gluon energy 36 %, trace anomaly 23 %, quark-mass term 9 %, at the physical pion mass (the text; the abstract rounds 33 % and 37 %))</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/0606016\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">F. Bissey et al., PRD 76, 114512 (2007)</a> (lattice QCD: the gluon flux tube of three quarks is Y-shaped, radius 0.38 &plusmn; 0.03 fm; quenched, heavy static quarks)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1006.5508\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">A. Ong, J. C. Berengut, V. V. Flambaum, PRC 82, 014320 (2010)</a> (eq. 8: the charge radius split into the point protons, the nucleons&rsquo; own sizes and the Darwin&ndash;Foldy term 0.033 fm&sup2;)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0806.2886\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">M. Centelles et al., PRL 102, 122502 (2009)</a> (quotes the antiprotonic-atom neutron-skin fit 0.90 I &minus; 0.03 fm of Trzci&nacute;ska et al., PRL 87, 082501 (2001))</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.4604\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">M. Diehl and P. Kroll, Eur. Phys. J. C 73, 2397 (2013)</a> (generalized parton distributions fitted to all the nucleon form factors: where up and down quarks of each momentum fraction sit, eq. 44 and table 11; the Accurate quark dots)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1202.2281\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">S. Alekhin, J. Bl&uuml;mlein and S. Moch (ABM11), PRD 86, 054009 (2012)</a> (parton densities, NLO grid from LHAPDF at 2 GeV: how much of the momentum each kind of parton carries, at which momentum fraction)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.02739\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">COMPASS, R. Akhunzyanov et al., PLB 793, 188 (2019)</a> (deeply virtual Compton scattering: partons at x &asymp; 0.03 spread 0.58 fm from the centre)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0907.5289\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">H1, F. D. Aaron et al., PLB 681, 391 (2009)</a> (deeply virtual Compton scattering: sea quarks and gluons at x &asymp; 0.001 spread 0.64 fm)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0507286\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">L. Frankfurt, M. Strikman and C. Weiss, Ann. Rev. Nucl. Part. Sci. 55, 403 (2005)</a> (the gluons' spread from J/&psi; photoproduction: &lang;&rho;&sup2;&rang; 0.28 fm&sup2; at x ~ 0.1, 0.35 fm&sup2; at small x)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ex/0602017\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">R. Bradford, A. Bodek, H. Budd and J. Arrington (BBBA05), Nucl. Phys. B Proc. Suppl. 159, 127 (2006)</a> (fits to the measured proton and neutron magnetic and proton electric form factors, Table 1: the Accurate quark clouds)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.10840\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">H. Atac et al., Nature Commun. 12, 1759 (2021)</a> (neutron charge radius &lang;r&sup2;&rang; = &minus;0.110 fm&sup2; and its fit of the neutron electric form factor, eq. 5: the Accurate quark clouds)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.3629\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">S. Venkat, J. Arrington, G. A. Miller and X. Zhan, PRC 83, 015203 (2011)</a> (an independent proton form factor fit: the quark clouds agree with it to 1 %)</li><li><a href=\"http