ef=\"https://spec.jpl.nasa.gov/ftp/pub/catalog/doc/d017002.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">JPL Molecular Spectroscopy catalogue, entry 17002 (ammonia; Yu, Drouin &amp; Pearson, 2010)</a> (ammonia&rsquo;s rotational constants B = 298,192.92 MHz and C = 186,695.86 MHz, 9.94662 and 6.22750 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>; CCCBDB&rsquo;s listed 9.4443 is a slip)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semi-empirical_mass_formula\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">J. W. Rohlf, 1994, Modern Physics from &alpha; to Z<sup>0</sup> (Wiley), through Wikipedia&rsquo;s table of the semi-empirical mass formula</a> (the Bethe&ndash;Weizs&auml;cker coefficients 15.75, 17.8, 0.711, 23.7 and 11.18 MeV, used only for a nucleus AME2020 does not list)</li><li>The colours of the spectral lines in the Atom Lab: the CIE 1931 2&deg; standard observer (colour-science 0.4.7, above) to sRGB (IEC 61966-2-1); a pure spectral colour is outside what a screen can show, so D65 white is mixed in until it fits, keeping its hue (0.52.1; until 0.52.0 Dan Bruton&rsquo;s RGB approximation).</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Distances of Polaris, Betelgeuse and Rigel are uncertain, so they are rounded.</div><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">The star masses are published estimates, in solar masses, and some are uncertain; the Fomalhaut and Arcturus masses come from stellar models, not from measuring an orbit. The numbers were taken from the papers&rsquo; summaries and have not been checked against their tables. Stars whose mass could not be sourced show none.</div><div class=\"srcH\">🌍 Spinning planets and dates</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10569-017-9805-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Archinal et al.: Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements, 2015</a> (the direction of each planet’s north pole and how fast it turns)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.sai.msu.ru/neb/Bidon/ChaptersPDF/nsd_App06b8_Rot.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The IAU rotational elements table (PDF)</a> (Mars to Pluto, checked against the numbers below)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/CS-SI/Orekit/blob/develop/src/main/java/org/orekit/bodies/PredefinedIAUPoles.java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orekit: the IAU pole and turning models as code</a> (Mercury, Venus, Earth and the small wobble terms of Mars and Neptune)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axial_tilt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: axial tilt</a> (to compare each planet’s lean)</li><li><a href=\"https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/questions/question60.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA StarChild: Mars in August 2003</a> (27 Aug 2003, 55.76 million km)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_conjunction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: great conjunction</a> (Jupiter and Saturn, 21 Dec 2020, 0.1° apart)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_transit_of_Venus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the 2012 transit of Venus</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halley%27s_Comet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Halley’s Comet</a> (closest on 9 Feb 1986, due back 28 Jul 2061)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discovery_of_Neptune\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the discovery of Neptune</a> (23 Sep 1846)</li></ul><div class=\"lrnP lrnMuted\">Planet places on other dates come from the NASA/JPL orbit numbers listed under Space and orbits.</div><div class=\"srcH\">☀️ The Sun, seasons and the timeline</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li>Galileo and Saturn&rsquo;s &ldquo;ears&rdquo; (1610), in the timeline: A. Van Helden, &ldquo;Saturn through the Telescope: The First Century&rdquo;, DPS 2005, BAAS 37 (3), abstract 639</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/CS-SI/Orekit/blob/develop/src/main/java/org/orekit/bodies/PredefinedIAUPoles.java\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Orekit: the IAU models as code</a> (the Sun’s pole, 286.13° and 63.87°, and its turning rate, 14.1844° a day)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_rotation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: solar rotation</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the Sun</a> (the equator turns faster than the poles; the axis leans 7.25°)</li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Earth facts</a> (the 23.4° tilt, and that the tilt, not the distance, causes the seasons)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apsis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: apsis</a> (Earth’s closest and farthest distance from the Sun, with the 2026 dates)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solstice\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: solstice</a> (the 2026 equinox and solstice times)</li><li>Saturn’s ring: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christiaan_Huygens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Christiaan Huygens</a> and <a href=\"https://dwc.knaw.nl/en/?p=2435\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">the Huygens ring discovery (KNAW)</a></li><li>The Sun is not the centre of the galaxy: <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Harlow-Shapley\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Britannica: Harlow Shapley</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_Center\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Galactic Center</a></li><li>Landing on Venus and Mars: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venera_7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Venera 7</a>, <a href=\"https://solarviews.com/eng/venera7.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Solarviews: Venera 7</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viking_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Viking 1</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-1/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Viking 1</a> (the sources disagree on how long Venera 7 transmitted, so the text gives no number)</li><li>A comet hits Jupiter: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comet_Shoemaker%E2%80%93Levy_9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9</a> and <a href=\"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Comet-Shoemaker-Levy-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Britannica</a></li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Voyager_1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Voyager 1</a> (leaving the Sun’s wind, 25 Aug 2012, 121 AU)</li><li>Comet landing and Ceres: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_(spacecraft)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Rosetta</a>, <a href=\"https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/rosettas-philae-makes-historic-first-landing-on-a-comet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA/JPL: Philae’s landing</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawn_(spacecraft)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Dawn</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Dawn</a></li><li>Touching the Sun: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parker_Solar_Probe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: Parker Solar Probe</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/parker-solar-probe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NASA: Parker Solar Probe</a> (the corona crossing was on 28 Apr 2021 and announced in Dec 2021)</li><li>Weighing the black hole: <a href=\"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2020/press-release/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S2_(star)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia: the star S2</a></li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">Molecule Lab</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://cccbdb.nist.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Computational Chemistry Comparison and Benchmark Database (CCCBDB)</a>, NIST Standard Reference Database 101, Release 22: the experimental geometries (bond lengths and angles) and experimental rotational constants of the 15 molecules, each value with the reference code CCCBDB gives (1979HUB/HER, NISTdiatomic, 1966Herzberg, 1979Hoy/Bun:1, 1979Hir:213, 1974sve/kov, 1998Kuc, Gurvich, 1955Ven/Gor:1200, 1984Her/Mes:42), read on 18 Oct 2026; and each molecule&rsquo;s fastest measured vibration (the CCCBDB values below), for the note that a molecule vibrates all the time (period 1 / (c &times; wavenumber); water: 3756 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, 8.9 fs). Methanol's H-C-O angle is not in CCCBDB: it is derived from its H-C-H angle, assuming three equivalent C-H bonds, and marked as derived.</li><li><a href=\"https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/Compositions/stand_alone.pl\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST: Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions</a> (the masses of <sup>1</sup>H, <sup>12</sup>C, <sup>14</sup>N, <sup>16</sup>O and <sup>35</sup>Cl, read on 18 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/periodic-table/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PubChem periodic table</a> (CPK colours and van der Waals radii: drawing conventions, a molecule has no hard edge; ball-and-stick ball sizes are only a drawing scale)</li><li>Molecule Lab, Realistic style (0.21): the electron density and the electrostatic potential of the 15 molecules are CALCULATED, not measured, by this project&rsquo;s tool (<code>native/tools/make_density_data.py</code>, run once; the app only reads its results): Kohn&ndash;Sham density functional theory with <a href=\"https://pyscf.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PySCF</a> 2.14.0 (Q. Sun et al., J. Chem. Phys. 153, 024109 (2020)), the B3LYP functional (A. D. Becke, J. Chem. Phys. 98, 5648 (1993); C. Lee, W. Yang and R. G. Parr, Phys. Rev. B 37, 785 (1988); PySCF&rsquo;s &ldquo;b3lyp&rdquo;, libxc functional 402) and the aug-cc-pVTZ basis set (T. H. Dunning Jr., J. Chem. Phys. 90, 1007 (1989); R. A. Kendall, T. H. Dunning Jr. and R. J. Harrison, J. Chem. Phys. 96, 6796 (1992)), DFT grid level 3, at exactly the measured (CCCBDB) shapes above. O<sub>2</sub> is a triplet (unrestricted, total density of both spins); ozone is treated as closed-shell, an approximation (it has multi-reference character). The computed dipoles are compared with the measured ones from <a href=\"https://cccbdb.nist.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST CCCBDB</a> (debye): HCl 1.093 (NISTDiatomic), HCN 2.980 (NSRDS-NBS10), H<sub>2</sub>O 1.857 (1991Sho/Ebe:5875-5882), O<sub>3</sub> 0.530 (NSRDS-NBS10), NH<sub>3</sub> 1.476 (1970Shi:3572-3576), H<sub>2</sub>CO 2.332 (2003The/Cal:8910-8915), CH<sub>3</sub>OH 1.672 (1981Sas/Lee:228-230); H<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>, CO<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>2</sub>, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> and C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> 0 by symmetry (NSRDS-NBS10); given to this job by the owner as text, read on 18 Oct 2026. The surface is a chosen density cut-off: 0.0016 e/bohr<sup>3</sup> matched measured molecular surfaces best in A. Alibakhshi and L. V. Schäfer, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-50408-8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nature Communications 15, 6086 (2024)</a>: 104 molecules, 1.6 % mean error (0.002 gave 3.2 %, 0.001 gave 7.0 %; that paper credits 0.002 to Bader and co-workers and 0.001 to Boyd). Units: the Bohr radius, the hartree and the debye from CODATA 2022. Pictures of real molecules by atomic force microscopy (L. Gross, F. Mohn, N. Moll, P. Liljeroth and G. Meyer, IBM Research Zurich, Science 325, 1110 (2009): a tip ending in a CO molecule over pentacene) show forces, not the electron density.</li><li>Molecule Lab, Vibrations (0.23): the normal modes, their harmonic wavenumbers and their infrared intensities are CALCULATED, not measured, by this project&rsquo;s tool (<code>native/tools/make_vib_data.py</code>, run once): the same PySCF 2.14.0, B3LYP and aug-cc-pVTZ as above (a first run with aug-cc-pVDZ put the bends of acetylene, HCN and benzene 8&ndash;20&nbsp;% low; benzene&rsquo;s Hessian by central differences of the analytic gradient, using its symmetry), the analytic Hessian with the most abundant isotopes&rsquo; masses (NIST, above), at the measured (CCCBDB) shapes, and the dipole derivatives by central differences (0.005 bohr). The intensity of a band is N<sub>A</sub>/(12&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>c<sup>2</sup>) |d&mu;/dQ|<sup>2</sup> (the standard harmonic formula, e.g. E. B. Wilson, J. C. Decius and P. C. Cross, Molecular Vibrations, 1955; not opened in this session). Harmonic wavenumbers come out a few per cent above measured bands. The MEASURED wavenumbers, symmetries, descriptions and infrared intensities shown are from <a href=\"https://cccbdb.nist.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST CCCBDB</a> (experimental pages, read 4 Nov 2026, saved in <code>native/data/molecules/vib/cccbdb/</code>, each value with CCCBDB&rsquo;s reference code), with CCCBDB&rsquo;s vibrational scaling factor for B3LYP/aug-cc-pVTZ, 0.968 &plusmn; 0.019; paired with the calculated modes by <code>native/tools/make_vib_measured.py</code>. Two modes a harmonic calculation cannot fit are named in the app: methanol&rsquo;s torsion (on CCCBDB&rsquo;s &ldquo;Big anharmonicity&rdquo; list) and ammonia&rsquo;s umbrella, which the <a href=\"https://webbook.nist.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">NIST Chemistry WebBook</a> (T. Shimanouchi, Tables of Molecular Vibrational Frequencies, 1972) lists as two levels, 932.5 and 968.3 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup> (the inversion doublet). 0.41: the same tables give HCN&rsquo;s CN stretch in the gas, 2096.85 cm<sup>&minus;1</sup>, used in place of CCCBDB&rsquo;s 2089 (the liquid&rsquo;s Raman band). 0.34: the vibrational constants (&omega;<sub>e</sub>, &omega;<sub>e</sub>x<sub>e</sub> and the higher terms) of H<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub> and HCl for their measured ladder of levels, from K. P. Huber &amp; G. Herzberg, Constants of Diatomic Molecules (1979), as the WebBook gives them (read 6 Nov 2026). 0.53, rotation: the same tables&rsquo; B<sub>e</sub>, &alpha;<sub>e</sub> and D<sub>e</sub> (N<sub>2</sub> D<sub>0</sub>; O<sub>2</sub> B<sub>0</sub> and D<sub>0</sub>; 0.54.1: HCl&rsquo;s footnotes 29 and 30, &gamma;<sub>e</sub> and the change of D with v), and DCl&rsquo;s page (read 14 Nov 2026) to check the Born&ndash;Oppenheimer mass rules that scale H<sup>35</sup>Cl to its isotopes; HCl&rsquo;s measured fundamental lines from D. H. Rank, D. P. Eastman, B. S. Rao &amp; T. A. Wiggins, J. Opt. Soc. Am. 52, 1 (1962), Table III, and its band origins up to v = 5 from the same paper (the Birge&ndash;Sponer analysis); the bond lengths r<sub>e</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>&rsquo;s dissociation energy (footnote 125) from Huber &amp; Herzberg&rsquo;s pages; the dissociation energies of N<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub> and HCl from B. deB. Darwent, Bond Dissociation Energies in Simple Molecules, NSRDS-NBS 31 (National Bureau of Standards, 1970), the recommended values; the nuclear-spin weights from the nuclear spins (NUBASE2020) by the rule in G. Herzberg, Spectra of Diatomic Molecules (1950); the masses and abundances of <sup>1</sup>H, <sup>2</sup>H, <sup>35</sup>Cl and <sup>37</sup>Cl from NIST&rsquo;s Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions. The swing&rsquo;s size is the classical turning point of the lowest level, &radic;(&hbar;/&omega;) in the mass-weighted normal coordinate; constants CODATA 2022. Molecule Lab, the vibrating density (0.24, water): the density at both ends of each swing of the &ldquo;3 times bigger&rdquo; picture, by <code>native/tools/make_vib_density.py</code> with the same PySCF, B3LYP and aug-cc-pVTZ as the still density, on its grids; blended quadratically between them in the app. CALCULATED. Ozone&rsquo;s values are less certain (closed-shell B3LYP for a molecule with multi-reference character): its symmetric stretch comes out with almost no infrared band.</li></ul><div class=\"srcH\">⚫ Black Hole Lab</div><ul class=\"srcL\"><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.07788\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">C. N. A. Willmer, ApJS 236, 47 (2018)</a> (the Sun&rsquo;s apparent V magnitude, &minus;26.76 (Table 3): the bridge that puts the stars on the disc&rsquo;s scale under True brightness)</li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/astronexus/HYG-Database/blob/main/hyg/README.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The HYG Database README (D. Nash)</a> (the catalogue&rsquo;s magnitudes are V magnitudes)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09103\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">S. Bar&aacute;, C. Bao-Varela, Int. J. Sustainable Lighting (2023)</a> (the CIE general disability glare equation, its range and the average observer: the eye&rsquo;s glare)</li><li><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.zemedi.2012.10.009\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">T. J. T. P. van den Berg, L. Franssen, B. Kruijt, J. E. Coppens, Z. Med. Phys. 23, 6 (2013)</a> (the same glare equation (CIE 135/1999, equation 11, by J. J. Vos and T. J. T. P. van den Berg), checked against the first)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03808\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">O. James, E. von Tunzelmann, P. Franklin, K. S. Thorne, Class. Quantum Grav. 32, 065001 (2015)</a> (the space round a black hole, spinning or not, the hovering camera, the light&rsquo;s blue shift and the ray equations; the step-by-step ray integrator every closed form is checked against; which light comes from the horizon round a spinning hole, without following it; the Film look: the film&rsquo;s spin 0.6, its disc at one temperature of 4,500 K and its edges, no colour or brightness shift and its white balance, sections 4.1 to 4.3, Figures 13 to 16 and appendix A.6)</li><li><a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1972ApJ...178..347B\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">J. M. Bardeen, W. H. Press, S. A. Teukolsky, Astrophys. J. 178, 347 (1972)</a> (the horizon, the photon orbits and the innermost stable orbit for any spin: 2, 3 and 6 GM/c&sup2; without spin; the ergosphere, inside which nothing can stand still)</li><li><a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1979A%26A....75..228L\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">J.-P. Luminet, Astron. Astrophys. 75, 228 (1979)</a> (the light paths of a non-spinning hole in closed form (elliptic integrals); the picture of a thin disc round it, its rings and its colour shift; the observed total light as the 4th power of the shift, for Light: All light)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.12881\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">S. E. Gralla, A. Lupsasca, Phys. Rev. D 101, 044032 (2020)</a> (the light paths that start inside the critical one, in closed form; from 0.55.9 every light path round the spinning hole, in closed form: no step-by-step integration)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.12873\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">S. E. Gralla, A. Lupsasca, Phys. Rev. D 101, 044031 (2020)</a> (the critical impact parameter 3&radic;3 GM/c&sup2;; each further image of a star fainter by e<sup>&minus;&pi;</sup>, or by the factor of a spinning hole&rsquo;s photon orbits; a spinning hole&rsquo;s shadow seen from far away)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.05810\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">R. Berens, D. O. Chang, T. Gravely, A. Lupsasca, Publ. Astron. Soc. Pac. 138, 114501 (2026)</a> (the light path&rsquo;s equation in the form the lab uses; the same closed forms worked out for every pixel)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.07101\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">V. Perlick, O. Yu. Tsupko, Phys. Rep. 947, 1 (2022)</a> (the shadow&rsquo;s size for a camera at any distance (Synge&rsquo;s formula); the sizes of a spinning hole&rsquo;s shadow seen from far away)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1511.06025\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">A. Riazuelo, Int. J. Mod. Phys. D 28, 1950042 (2019)</a> (one bending curve for every pixel; the images of point stars and their brightness; the hovering camera&rsquo;s clock and blue shift)</li><li><a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1974ApJ...191..499P\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">D. N. Page, K. S. Thorne, Astrophys. J. 191, 499 (1974)</a> (the thin gas disc: how bright each ring is, its inner edge at the innermost stable orbit, and that its light is the efficiency times the feeding rate)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1104.5499\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">M. A. Abramowicz, P. C. Fragile, Living Rev. Relativ. 16, 1 (2013)</a> (the thin disc in numbers; the Eddington luminosity; above about 0.3 of it the thin-disc model no longer applies)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0586\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">F. Yuan, R. Narayan, Annu. Rev. Astron. Astrophys. 52, 529 (2014)</a> (where thin discs are found, down to about 1.5 % of the Eddington luminosity, and their temperatures, 10,000 to 10 million kelvin; why Sagittarius A*&rsquo;s gas is a faint hot fog instead)</li><li><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0201001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">J. Goodman, Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 339, 937 (2003)</a> (where thin discs round massive black holes end: about a thousand Schwarzschild radii, 0.01 parsec, beyond which the disc&rsquo;s own gravity breaks it up; the disc&rsquo;s outer edge, 2000 GM/c&sup2;)</l